A||||12||fierogt88||questions on 88 coupes||||5||
Z||000000||fierogt88||10-31-2000||01:19 AM||||were all the 88 coupes 4 bangers? Did the 88 coupes have the new "lotus-inspired" suspension?  If not is it the same suspension from the earlier models and can it use all the aftermarket suspension mods out there?<P>------------------<BR>88GT Red 50k||||reg||5
Z||000001||mrfiero||10-31-2000||01:36 AM||||Yes and no.  It is generally accepted that an '88 coupe is a 4 cyl., but the Formula is basically a coupe with a V-6 option.  It is easier to just say Formula, coupe and GT when describing '88 Fieros.....Formula and GT come standard with V-6 engines and the coupe has the 4 banger.<P>There is a big argument about Lotus having anything to do with the '88 suspension (they didn't)......that said, all '88s have the same suspension.  The Formulas and GT's are 100% identical in the suspension department.  The 4 bangers are the same, but have no rear sway bar and they have different spring rates.   All '88 suspension parts are interchangable though, so the differences in bushings/springs, etc are negligible.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Cooter||10-31-2000||04:19 PM||||All 88's are interchangeable with another 88, not with other years without some modification. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||TheFiero64||10-31-2000||04:41 PM||||My 88 coupe has a V6. The greatest thing I ever installed. <P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/TheFiero64_signature1.gif"> ||||reg||10
Z||000004||Master_Sushi||10-31-2000||06:19 PM||||I have an 88 coupe. The suspension is really nice. I havent done much driving in any other fieros then mine, So im really not sure how much differend it is from pre 88. But what I do know is its a pain if you run into any probs with an 88. Im trying to find a front hub right now. And have had no luck yet. But I still would take an 88 over a pre 88.||||reg||1
Z||000005||mrfiero||10-31-2000||07:20 PM||||Finding parts for '88 Fieros isn't as difficult as you think....just ask!<P>I have 2 '88 parts cars and I know several others on this forum have some too.  Which hub do you need....left or right?  E-mail me.....I have them!||||reg||1
Z||000006||Tom88gt||10-31-2000||07:32 PM||||If the front hub your looking for is the front wheel bearing assembly...many auto parts stores have these.  $85 here, I've already priced, just in case.  And your can't adjust the bearing or re-pack them either...sealed unit.||||reg||12
Z||000007||Songman||10-31-2000||09:56 PM||||MrFiero is right about just asking if you are looking for 88 parts! I just got back from Advance Auto Parts where I bought two front brake rotors for a Cavalier for $13.99 each! I was sure dreading having to pay over $50 each for the Fiero rears! All I had to do was ask and I found out how to save a lot of money! Unfortunately, I don't think you can do that with the fronts from what I learned when I asked... Good luck to you..||||reg||14
Z||000008||Fformula88||10-31-2000||11:14 PM||||About the 88 Front Hub assembly:<P>These are sealed units, so there is no re-packing of bearings.  They are also very hard to find new... AC Delco has discontinued them.  However, in an email correspondance with the Fiero Store (this is not a plug), they told me they have found someone to manufacture new hub assemblies.  They expect them to be available in 3-4 months.  This info is a couple weeks old.  <P>If you need one now... try getting a good used one to hold you over.  There are a number of people on the forum that should be able to get you one.  Overall, the 88's brake/suspension parts are a little more expensive, but compared to late model cars they don't seem that bad.  My 97 Probe's brake rotors/calipers etc are a little more than the Fiero's!||||reg||1
Z||000009||ChadMan||11-01-2000||08:58 AM||||As somebody who uses an 88 Fiero for daily transportation, I find stories like these scary.  After 115K, my front wheel bearings seem fine (no noise, no play in the wheels), but I am getting sick of the 1-week wait for mail-order parts anytime I have to service the suspension or brakes when parts for the 84-87 are as close as NAPA or Autozone.<P>I may own more 88 Fieros, but I doubt I'll ever use one as daily transportation again.||||reg||9
Z||000010||Master_Sushi||11-01-2000||10:52 AM||||Im going to try a few other auto stores about the hub today. If I cant find it, ill get a used one off the forum for sure. Thanks for all the info!||||reg||1
Z||000011||Raydar||11-01-2000||02:27 PM||||I got my 88 front hub/bearing from Advance Auto. Had to be ordered, but only took 1 day. Was <I>obscenely</I> expensive, ($100+) though. <BR>They also ordered rear brake calipers for me. Beat Fiero Store's price by a bundle.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar<P>"If I have to fake 'genuine concern' one more time today, I'm gonna scream!" -TCH||||reg||1
Z||000012||Master_Sushi||11-01-2000||04:13 PM||||Ok, I give up on finding a new one. The only place that I can find that *might* have one is a pontiac dealership. They can order one for $230. But I hear fron the fiero store and a few auto places that they arnt being made anymore. So I cant really trust anyone if they say they can order them. So ill jsut try and find a used one.<P>I sent you an email mrfiero.||||reg||1
